186045417] [17592186133983 #edb/ref 1045 "Inner Product Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133983 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133983 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133983 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133983 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "02348ec0-92a1-55fa-a513-9148a7a85088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2].$ Find $⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71904bfc-c560-50f5-ac2b-41eb0a528292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134385 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134632 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134632 #edb/ref 1061 20189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134632 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30a6ea64-4bb3-5509-b69d-433ee390d90f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134632 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134633 #edb/ref 1061 20190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2cb651dc-e28c-599d-bca7-600e9e22174f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134634 #edb/ref 1061 20191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb70017f-2feb-562e-9c18-92b37a4a40ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134635 #edb/ref 1061 20192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6655ce76-39d7-5522-a425-3a237edac607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134747 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134747 #edb/ref 1061 23793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134747 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55c93c2b-8932-5053-b4c4-297bb2faf2ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134747 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134748 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134748 #edb/ref 1061 23794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134748 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71837f9d-0b3c-5b65-af89-30a509dbded1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134748 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134749 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134749 #edb/ref 1061 23795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134749 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db12ef78-1aac-5236-9cfa-e2a0ed77fabc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134749 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135033 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $V$ be a vector space. Any function $f:V \\times V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that takes two vectors $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in V$ and returns a real number is an **inner product** on $V,$ denoted $\\langle \\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\rangle,$ if the following axioms are satisfied:\n\n- Linearity in the first argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & ⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩,\\,\\alpha \\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n- Positive definiteness:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle \\gt 0\\quad \\textrm{for}\\quad  \\mathbf a\\neq  \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0\\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThe concept of an inner product is a generalization of the dot product. It allows us to apply the geometric ideas of length and angle to abstract vector spaces. \n\nNotice that the standard dot product on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ indeed satisfies these axioms. Recall that if $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v \\in \\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf u, \\mathbf v\\rangle = \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = u_1\\cdot v_1 + u_2\\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n\\cdot v_n .\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v, \\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb R.$ Let's now check that our three axioms are satisfied. \n\n- We first show that the dot product is linear in the first argument. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u+v,w⟩ & =(u+v)\\cdot w \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =⟨u,w⟩+⟨v,w⟩\\,✓ \\\\ ⟨\\alpha u,v⟩ & =(\\alpha u)\\cdot v \\\\  & =(\\alpha u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha (u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha ⟨u,v⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we show that the dot product is symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,v⟩ & =u\\cdot v \\\\  & =v\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨v,u⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we show that the dot product is positive definite:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,u⟩ & =u\\cdot u \\\\  & ={u}_{1}{u}_{1}+{u}_{2}{u}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}{u}_{n} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is clearly positive for all $\\mathbf u\\neq\\mathbf 0$ and is zero for $\\mathbf u = \\mathbf 0 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSince the dot product satisfies our three axioms, we conclude that it is an inner product. \n\nFinally, note that since an inner product is both linear in the first argument and symmetric, then it is also linear in the second argument." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135033 #edb/ref 1065 3604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135033 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5640068c-e4b6-5268-9c3b-d2c6f0906725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135033 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135077 #edb/ref 1064 "The first type of inner product that we will discuss is the **weighted dot product**. \n\nGiven a sequence of positive real numbers $d_1, d_2, \\ldots d_n > 0,$ called \"weights\", the function\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^n d_i x_i y_i\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is similar to the dot product, except here, different components are given different weights." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135077 #edb/ref 1065 3992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135077 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "97a9d9a1-c4ef-55fb-ab09-32189a73c2ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135077 #edb/ref 1067 "The Weighted Dot Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135078 #edb/ref 1064 "The next inner product that we will discuss concerns inner products on polynomials.\n\nGiven a sequence of distinct real numbers ![](images/52/52cae406fa2952a80a1bab7e02d5667b3acda56e4b2456a3dcb6600a91105482.svg) the function\n![](images/d1/d1f9dd085bdf1f3d49f99a26d17d75a4e1bd1ee413fff06bfff4198e4e1ee59d.svg)\ndefines an inner product on ![](images/35/353273654347dcd414aa60782023eff4d676ea7f598a8d2f9c923ee6c4cba811.svg) the vector space of polynomials of degree at most ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg)\n\nLet's consider a few examples:\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/ba/ba47d908e29aa7475933ff0536d610abe1e3752dd165c8cf6f655f83ae48156b.svg)\n![](images/24/24d15f48f890f3bff207460d175db7d16b35fd21a05e1dc3d1103cde1453c531.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/50/507c62e239664ab41c74f85c0e905c0a84d3da5183991232db789ce8a9d385f1.svg)\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/c5/c5c6bb73a744c88af37d2cbce6ac9e27957fc77dbcddb4ec6fb04fb02ac1bbc2.svg)\n![](images/5f/5f66a28d825609fe3e9c6dca065cb3f96e6fe0fafccd56ff87d7608fa7cf35af.svg)\n\nNotice that this inner product on ![](images/29/297749fafabb3d9d253c58491c364d113f5579eacda31a07a8b8c792cc9d7777.svg) requires summing ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) terms. This is essential to ensure positive definiteness. For example, the function\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\nis *not* an inner product on ![](images/68/686b2d83c1abe018b786ecc5f7c624a4932fda35b57d09ca5385a41f8a3bb233.svg) since there exist nonzero polynomials ![](images/aa/aa374f18ad64a3743718af3bbcef010a756b25dae566ccb9413a790a97b72067.svg) such that ![](images/d1/d138796b5b181d78264c70ef5c9b68db4dba9c6603612cca303c87e09924ec2d.svg) which violates the condition ![](images/63/631ddd9e222592497d44379cd2d6b6aac118ff1a0e4c6587347473112aae26c5.svg)\n\nIn general, a nonzero polynomial of degree at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) can have at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) distinct roots, so vanishing at ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) distinct points forces the polynomial to be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135078 #edb/ref 1065 3993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135078 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e88c3857-ea1b-58ee-8584-5e787b53f7f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135078 #edb/ref 1067 "An Inner Product of Polynomials in One Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135079 #edb/ref 1064 "Our final inner product is a generalization of what we saw in the previous example. The function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathcal{C}[a,b],$ the vector space of continuous functions on $[a,b].$ \n\nHere, instead of finding the product of the functions at some discrete points and summing them, we sum (integrate) the product of the functions over the whole interval $[a,b].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135079 #edb/ref 1065 3994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135079 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9d7e7a9f-6893-5af3-b47e-e5ef2ccba9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135079 #edb/ref 1067 "An Inner Product of Functions in One Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327215 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20ba600e-0358-513c-9008-d4164bd322af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327533 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327533 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327533 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44900815-93d0-5419-bda4-15f71f1c2ebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328015 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328015 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328015 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d5d4c0c-e9e9-5fde-8a0d-8854cd85cbf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328165 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{t}+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328165 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328165 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e2a67c3-6516-5ef0-8910-205fb5e42518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328174 #ed